-- title:
--   Finite separable extensions of K(x) are curves over K
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $x \in F$ be transcendental over $K$, i.e. the evaluation map from $K[X]$ to $F$ at $x$ is injective. Assume that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {x}` and that the extension $F/K(x)$ is separable. Then `IsCurveOver K F` holds, that is: (i) for every $f \in F$ with $f \neq 0$ there is a divisor $D \in$ `Divisor K F` whose value at every place $v$ of $F/K$ is the order $v.\mathrm{ord}\, f$ and whose degree is $0$ (the content of `HasPrincipalDivisors`); (ii) for every place $v$ of $F/K$ the residue field of $v$ is a finite-dimensional $K$-module; and (iii) the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega_{F/K}$ is a free $F$-module with $\operatorname{finrank}_F \Omega_{F/K} = 1$. Here a place of $F/K$ is a valuation subring $\mathcal{O}_v$ of $F$ containing the image of $K$, not equal to all of $F$, and whose ideals are all principal.
--
--   This is the statement that a field extension generated by a transcendental element together with a finite separable extension is the function field of a curve over $K$, in the sense of the axiom package `IsCurveOver`: principal divisors of degree zero, finite residue degrees at all places, and a one-dimensional space of differentials. It supplies the curve hypothesis for the many later results about divisors, Picard groups and Jacobians of such function fields.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_isCurveOver_of_transcendental_of_isSeparable.lean

